        "original_id": "87fad72c-54f4-5550-9742-ed43ec1d630a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jamie Bell, Steven Gould, Ed Whitmore\n- Works: Billy Elliot, King Kong, Jumper, The Adventures of Tintin, Snowpiercer, Turn: Washington's Spies, Hallam Foe\n- Awards: BAFTA for Best Actor in a Leading Role\n- Topics: film adaptations, acting careers\n\nFacts:\n- Hallam Foe was published on August 11, 2001.\n- Billy Elliot was released in 2000, and Jamie Bell rose to prominence for his debut role in that film.\n- Jamie Bell won the BAFTA for Best Actor in a Leading Role for Billy Elliot.\n- Jumper is a 2008 American science fiction action film loosely based on Steven Gould's 1992 novel of the same name.\n- The Hallam Foe film adaptation by Ed Whitmore starred Jamie Bell and was released in the UK on August 31, 2007.\n- Jamie Bell is an English actor and dancer born on 14 March 1986.\n- Jamie Bell is also known for roles in King Kong (2005), Jumper (2008), The Adventures of Tintin (2011), and Snowpiercer (2013).\n- Jamie Bell starred as Abraham Woodhull in the TV series Turn: Washington's Spies in 2014.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4b2599bb-5147-5d44-b787-35deb1595356",
        "text": "Page: Jamie Bell. Sentence: Andrew James Matfin Bell (born 14 March 1986) is an English actor and dancer who rose to prominence for his debut role in \"Billy Elliot\" (2000) for which he won the BAFTA for Best Actor in a Leading Role.\nPage: Jamie Bell. Sentence: He is also known for his roles in the films \"King Kong\" (2005), \"Jumper\" (2008), \"The Adventures of Tintin\" (2011), and \"Snowpiercer\" (2013), as well as starring as Abraham Woodhull in the TV series, \"\" (2014).\nPage: Jumper (2008 film). Sentence: Jumper is a 2008 American science fiction action film loosely based on the 1992 science fiction novel of the same name written by Steven Gould.\nPage: Hallam Foe (novel). Sentence: It was published on August 11, 2001 and has inspired a film adaptation by Ed Whitmore, by the same name, which stars Jamie Bell, and was released in the UK on August 31, 2007.",
